/// Static, human- and agent-readable documentation of the public backend API.
/// The document is a compile-time literal: it reads no actor state and is
/// therefore safe to serve from a query function.
mixin () {
  /// Return the backend API documentation as Markdown.
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# School Management Backend API\n\nBackend for a school management system (ប្រព័ន្ធគ្រប់គ្រងសាលារៀន). It stores students, teachers, classes, and student-to-class enrollments, and exposes read endpoints for every signed-in caller plus admin-only mutation endpoints.\n\n## Authentication and identity\n\n- Sign-in is by **Internet Identity**. The frontend pins an Internet Identity **derivation origin**, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent that already holds the user's Internet Identity authorization must derive the per-app principal against that origin, for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`. A delegation obtained this way acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires.\n- A principal derived against a different origin is a **different principal** than the one the app's frontend registered, so it will not carry the role the user expects.\n- Registration happens only when a caller signs in through the app's own frontend. A principal that never did so is **unregistered**, even when it belongs to the app's owner. A direct API caller must register first by calling `_initialize_access_control` once as a signed-in caller, before any role-guarded call (guarded queries included). The first initializer receives the `#admin` role; subsequent callers receive the default `#user` role.\n- An unregistered or anonymous caller hitting a guarded endpoint receives a trap from the authorization layer (for example `Unauthorized: Only admins can create students` on a mutation, or the access-control layer's own unregistered-caller trap on a guarded read).\n\n## Authorization\n\n- **Reads** (`listStudents`, `getStudent`, `listTeachers`, `getTeacher`, `listClasses`, `getClass`, `listEnrollments`, `getDashboardStats`, `getRecentActivity`) require a signed-in caller. They are `query` calls and return data to any registered role.\n- **Mutations** (`createStudent`, `updateStudent`, `deleteStudent`, `createTeacher`, `updateTeacher`, `deleteTeacher`, `createClass`, `updateClass`, `deleteClass`, `addEnrollment`, `removeEnrollment`) are **admin only**. A non-admin caller traps with `Unauthorized: Only admins can <action> <entity>`.\n- `getApiDoc` is a public query and requires no role.\n\n## Units and encodings\n\n- **Identifiers** (`id`, `studentId`, `classId`, `homeroomTeacherId`) are `Nat` values assigned by the backend from monotonic counters. They are never reused, even after a delete.\n- **Timestamps** (`createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `enrolledAt`, `at`) are `Int` nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.\n- **`dateOfBirth`** is a plain `Text` ISO-8601 date (`YYYY-MM-DD`), not a timestamp.\n- **`gender`** is the variant `#male | #female | #other`.\n- **`status`** is the variant `#active | #inactive | #graduated | #transferred`.\n- **`classIds`** on a student is the list of classes the student is currently enrolled in; it is kept in sync with the enrollment records.\n- **`enrolledCount`** on a class is the number of current enrollments and is kept in sync with the enrollment records.\n- **`performedBy`** on an activity entry is the `Principal` that performed the action.\n- **`kind`** on an activity entry is one of `#studentCreated`, `#studentUpdated`, `#studentDeleted`, `#teacherCreated`, `#teacherUpdated`, `#teacherDeleted`, `#classCreated`, `#classUpdated`, `#classDeleted`, `#enrollmentAdded`, `#enrollmentRemoved`.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\n- All mutations are synchronous update calls: when the call resolves, the change is committed and visible to subsequent queries.\n- `getRecentActivity(limit)` returns the newest entries first. Poll it after a mutation to refresh an activity feed; there is no push channel.\n- `getDashboardStats()` returns aggregate counts (students, teachers, classes, enrollments) computed from current state.\n- `updateStudent`, `updateTeacher`, and `updateClass` return `null` when the id does not exist; `deleteStudent`, `deleteTeacher`, `deleteClass`, and `removeEnrollment` return `false` in that case. These are normal outcomes, not errors.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\n- Mutations are **not idempotent**. Retrying `createStudent`, `createTeacher`, `createClass`, or `addEnrollment` after a timeout creates a second record with a new id. Do not blind-retry a mutation whose result you did not observe; re-read the list first.\n- `updateStudent`, `updateTeacher`, and `updateClass` are safe to retry: they set fields to the supplied values and leave absent (`null`) fields unchanged.\n- `deleteStudent`, `deleteTeacher`, `deleteClass`, and `removeEnrollment` are safe to retry: a second call finds nothing and returns `false`.\n- `addEnrollment` is **not** idempotent and does not deduplicate: enrolling the same student into the same class twice creates two enrollment records and increments `enrolledCount` twice.\n\n## Errors, traps, and gotchas\n\n- `addEnrollment` traps with `Student not found` or `Class not found` when the referenced record does not exist, and with `Class is at full capacity` when `enrolledCount >= capacity`. Capacity is enforced on the backend; the frontend must not assume a slot is available.\n- `deleteStudent` cascades: it removes the student's enrollments and decrements the `enrolledCount` of every affected class.\n- `deleteClass` cascades: it removes the class's enrollments and removes the class id from every affected student's `classIds`.\n- `deleteTeacher` does **not** clear `homeroomTeacherId` on classes that reference the teacher; a class may keep a dangling homeroom teacher id.\n- `updateClass` can set `capacity` below the current `enrolledCount`; existing enrollments are not removed, but further `addEnrollment` calls will trap until the count drops.\n- All mutations record an entry in the activity feed, so `getRecentActivity` reflects admin actions.\n- The OQL endpoints `schema()` and `execute()` expose the `student`, `teacher`, `class`, and `enrollment` tables to the Data Intelligence agent; they are controller-only and are not part of the app's user-facing API.\n"
  };
};
